Q: UnsafeAccessor not updating private field when targeting a struct in .NET 8 A: <p>The reason for the observed behavior is the copy semantics of Value Type (<code>struct</code>) and not a problem with the <code>UnsafeAccessor</code> itself.</p>
<p>Looking at the definition given:</p>
<pre><code> public extern static ref int GetX(GridPoint target);
</code></pre>
<p>The first parameter is defined as <code>GridPoint target</code> (passed by value). Since <code>GridPoint</code> is a struct, a defensive copy of the data on the stack is made when <code>GetX</code> is called. The <code>UnsafeAccessor</code> returns a reference to the <code>_x</code> field correctly, but it is a reference to the field in the copy, not the actual variable.</p>
<p>The value <code>99</code> is stored in the temporary copy, which is immediately discarded after the statement is executed.</p>
<p>To fix this, the target parameter needs to be defined with the <code>ref</code> keyword. This tells the runtime to pass a reference to the existing stack variable, instead of making a copy.</p>
<p><strong>Correct Code :</strong></p>
<pre><code>public class Program
{
    // The target parameter must be 'ref' to avoid copying the struct
    [UnsafeAccessor(UnsafeAccessorKind.Field, Name = &quot;_x&quot;)]
    public extern static ref int GetX(ref GridPoint target);

    public static void Main()
    {
        var p = new GridPoint(10);

        // Pass 'p' by reference
        GetX(ref p) = 99;

        Console.WriteLine($&quot;Expected: 99, Actual: {p.X}&quot;); 
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This ensures that the generated accessor works directly on the memory location of the original variable <code>p</code>.</p>
